  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Mixture models naturally appear in a number of quantitative-genetic settings, wherein the observed distribution is really a weighted sum of a number of underlying distributions. For example, when a major diallelic locus is segregating in a population, the phenotypic distribution is a weighted sum of the three distributions representing each major locus genotype (Chapter 13). The general likelihood function for a single observation z from the kinds of mixture models considered in this book has the form",
      "source_page": 875,
      "printed_page": 855,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_appendix1:p057:b5",
