    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "where the distribution is assumed to result from $N$ underlying normals, the $k$th of which has frequency $\\pi_k$, mean $\\mu_k$, and common variance $\\sigma^2$. We assume that the number $N$ of underlying distributions is known and wish to estimate the $2N \\times 1$ vector $\\Theta$ of the $N$ means, the common variance, and the $N-1$ independent mixing proportions (the $\\pi_k$). With $n$ individuals independently drawn from this distribution, the full likelihood is",
      "source_page": 876,
      "printed_page": 856,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Genetics_appendix1:p058:b5"
      ],
      "bbox": [
